-- title:
--   §1 — a relation is of type I exactly when, up to a null set, X is a countable union of Borel sets on which it is trivial
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $X$ is a standard Borel space, $\mu$ a $\sigma$-finite measure on $X$, and $R \subseteq X \times X$ a discrete measured equivalence relation for $\mu$ (`IsDiscreteMeasured`): a Borel equivalence relation whose classes are countable and for which $\mu$ is quasi-invariant (the saturation of a null Borel set is null).
--
--   Then $R$ is of type I (`IsTypeI`: off a conull $R$-saturated Borel set, the quotient by $R$ is a standard Borel space) if and only if there are Borel sets $Y_0, Y_1, \dots$ whose union is conull and on each of which $R$ is trivial: two points of the same $Y_n$ that are $R$-equivalent are equal.
-- source:
--   Connes, A., Feldman, J., Weiss, B., An amenable equivalence relation is generated by a single transformation, Ergodic Theory Dynam. Systems 1 (1981) 431–450, https://doi.org/10.1017/S014338570000136X, p. 434, §1

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ConnesFeldmanWeiss

namespace ConnesFeldmanWeiss

theorem isTypeI_iff_exists_iUnion_trivial {X : Type*} [MeasurableSpace X]
    [StandardBorelSpace X] (μ : MeasureTheory.Measure X) [MeasureTheory.SigmaFinite μ]
    (R : Set (X × X)) (hR : IsDiscreteMeasured μ R) :
    IsTypeI μ R ↔ ∃ Y : ℕ → Set X, (∀ n, MeasurableSet (Y n)) ∧ μ (⋃ n, Y n)ᶜ = 0 ∧
      ∀ n x y, x ∈ Y n → y ∈ Y n → (x, y) ∈ R → x = y := by
  sorry

end ConnesFeldmanWeiss
